concept :the_false_form do
  name "The False Form"
  path "player/cosmology/the-false-form.md"
  tags :resonance, :cosmology, :religion, :legend
  prominence :renowned
  registry true
  aka "the Rhomboid", "the Outside Figure", "the Open Thing"

  prose <<~PROSE
    The False Form is the third figure of the #{ref :the_three_forms, "Three Forms"} — the impossible rhombus that the triptych draws between the lawful Tetrahedron and the lawful Triangulum and labels, in every honest copy, *false, outside.* It is the figure whose edges meet in ways no real edge can: a path that comes back to where it began and still refuses to close. The old caption beneath it is the one no apprentice forgets — *it is, and is not, all at once.*

    Of the three figures, the False Form is the most often drawn and the least often believed. It is not a thing anyone expects to meet. It is an idea, kept around because it is useful: the cleanest way to teach what coherence *is* is to set beside it the one shape that cannot have any. The False Form is the worked example of failure — the proof that contradicts itself, the figure you point to when you want to say *not that.*
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :significance, heading: "The Figure of Speech"
    In daylight the False Form has thinned into a turn of phrase. To call a deal *rhomboid* is to say it looks sound and isn't — a contract that reads closed and opens the moment weight goes on it. To say someone *draws the open thing* is to accuse them of a lie polished to look like a truth. Preachers of every register use the False Form the way a builder uses a cracked beam: as the example that makes the rule obvious. Falsehood that wears the shape of fact; entropy patient under everything built; the slow yawning-open of any figure left untended. The False Form is the name the system has agreed to give all of that at once, and almost no one who uses the word believes there is anything behind it but the word.

    The closing line of the #{ref :the_three_forms, "Three Forms"} is usually read as the last word on the matter, and it is meant to be reassuring: *Order is not enough. Truth is more. But falsehood is not.* The False Form, for all that it shimmers and pulls the eye in the picture, is held to have no real being of its own. It is the open dressed as the closed — an absence in the costume of a figure. It cannot *make* anything, the comfortable reading goes; it can only fail to be made. A figure of speech, a teaching tool, a devil drawn on the bottom of a card to make the rest of the card mean something.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :perception, heading: "What the Bench Knows"
    There is a quieter relationship with the idea, and it belongs to the people who work closest to where figures come apart. #{ref :tuners, "Tuners"} who run benches near the #{ref :bloom_zones, "Bloom Zones"}, salvagers who handle raw crystal out of the #{ref :deep_shear, "deep Shear"}, anyone who has spent too long around #{ref :duskgrain, "duskgrain"} — they tend to use the word *rhomboid* a little less lightly than city folk do. Not because they have a theory. Because they have a feeling: that there are places and substances where a vibration genuinely *will not close*, where a tuned figure that holds anywhere else simply refuses to hold, and where a person who lingers starts to feel their own edges go uncertain in a way that the #{ref :fae, "fae"} would recognize and no one else wants to.

    The trade keeps its superstitions about it — you don't draw the open thing on a hull, you don't whistle its caption in a depleted field, you re-tune a piece of glass that's started to *shimmer wrong* and you don't ask it why. Scholars file all of this under nerves and bad air, and they are mostly content to leave it there. The False Form remains, officially, the figure that isn't: the one of the Three the world agrees it does not have to fear, because a thing that is not cannot be coming for anyone.
  PROSE
end
